<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gbaya people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gbaya, Baya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gbaya are a people of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-african-republic">Central African Republic</a> and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a>, mainly farmers, speaking Gbaya languages. In the 19th century many were raided or ruled by the Fulbe lamidates of Adamawa, such as Ngaoundéré. From 1928 to 1931 they rose against French forced labour and recruitment in the Kongo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wara">Wara</a> rebellion, led by the prophet Karnou, one of the largest revolts in colonial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-equatorial-africa">French Equatorial Africa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gbaya-people.jpg" alt="Gbaya people" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ms Sarah Welch · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gbaya_people_distribution_map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
